1
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:04,790
Hey.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,040
- Hey.     - Hey.

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:07,420
Listen, uh...

4
00:00:07,590 --> 00:00:09,750
You think I have a chance with Janine?

5
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:11,840
Ugh, we've been through this.

6
00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:13,420
Don't do this to yourself.

7
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:16,010
She made it clear it isn't going to happen.

8
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:19,720
All right, then. I guess I shouldn't get too excited about the fact...

9
00:00:19,930 --> 00:00:21,520
that I just kissed her!

10
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:24,270
- Are you serious?     - That's great!

11
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:25,890
Yeah, well, we'll see.

12
00:00:27,810 --> 00:00:29,060
You kissed her?

13
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:32,030
Oh, we kissed it up real nice.

14
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:34,950
So you kissed. Then what happened?

15
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:37,950
I came over here to tell you guys.

16
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:40,870
So she's waiting over there for you?

17
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:44,160
Yeah.

18
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,950

19
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:27,240

20
00:01:29,830 --> 00:01:32,090
- I gotta go.     - Okay.

21
00:01:34,630 --> 00:01:36,760
I'm gonna be late for dance class.

22
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:38,680
Okay.

23
00:01:40,510 --> 00:01:43,180
- Okay, now I'm really leaving.     - All right.

24
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:44,520
But can you just...

25
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:46,890
just leave your lips?

26
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:50,900
- Bye.     - Bye.

27
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:54,900
Have you kissed her yet?

28
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:58,320
It's awesome!

29
00:01:58,530 --> 00:02:00,030
I could do it forever.

30
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,990
You know what? She kisses better than my mom cooks.

31
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:08,660
- I'm so glad you said "cooks."     - Right?

32
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,460
Rachel, you've got to tell the post office that you've moved.

33
00:02:13,630 --> 00:02:15,840
We still get your bills and stuff.

34
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:19,550
Oh! Oh! Pottery Barn! You can throw the rest away.

35
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:21,760
I'm not your garbage man.

36
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:25,060
I'm your mailman.

37
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:27,600
Monica, look.

38
00:02:27,770 --> 00:02:30,640
Here is that table that I ordered. Ah!

39
00:02:30,810 --> 00:02:35,110
- You got it from Pottery Barn?     - Yeah. It's an apothecary table.

40
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,650
Does anyone even know what an apothecary is?

41
00:02:37,820 --> 00:02:39,570
A pharmacist.

42
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:42,410
A pharmacist.

43
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:46,450
Phoebe hates Pottery Barn.

44
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:48,080
I hate Pottery Barn too.

45
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:51,500
They kicked me out just because I sat on a bed.

46
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:54,380
You took off your pants and got under the sheets.

47
00:02:57,210 --> 00:02:58,380
I was tired.

48
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:02,220
Phoebe hates Pottery Barn?

49
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:04,430
She hates mass-produced stuff.

50
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:07,600
She thinks her furniture should have a story behind it.

51
00:03:07,770 --> 00:03:09,810
This has a story behind it.

52
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:14,400
They had to ship it all the way from the White Plains store.

53
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:19,400
It's got to be one-of-a-kind.

54
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:23,610
Like that God-awful ceramic fruit bowl she has on her counter?

55
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:26,620
Hey! I made that for her.

56
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:29,790
- You made pottery?     - Yeah.

57
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:32,870
I made it out of a fruit bowl I found in the garbage.

58
00:03:35,130 --> 00:03:38,500
If you put that in her apartment, you'll never hear the end of it.

59
00:03:38,670 --> 00:03:41,260
I'll tell her that it's an antique.

60
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:43,430
She doesn't have to know where it came from.

61
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:46,180
Ah, I love these little drawers.

62
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:49,430
Oh, look! It says that it holds 300 CDs.

63
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:52,890
Ah, just like the apothecary tables of yore.

64
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:00,980
So the farmer says:

65
00:04:01,150 --> 00:04:02,990
"That's not a cow, and you're not milking."

66
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:07,910
I'm so glad you're together.

67
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:11,410
We wanted a couple to go out with and now we have one.

68
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:13,500
We're a couple of couples.

69
00:04:14,670 --> 00:04:17,540
I had so much fun tonight. What a great restaurant!

70
00:04:17,710 --> 00:04:21,920
And Chandler, I can't believe I let you pay for this one.

71
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:25,680
Thanks again, man.

72
00:04:26,890 --> 00:04:30,060
If you come over tomorrow, I'll make pasta.

73
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:31,640
- That'd be great!     - Okay.

74
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:34,310
But at least let us bring the wine.

75
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:37,810
- You don't have to.     - Ah, I insist.

76
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:42,610
- You'll get the wine, right?     - Yeah.

77
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,240
- Okay, good night.     - See you tomorrow.

78
00:04:47,410 --> 00:04:49,120
Can't wait.

79
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:56,670
How are we going to get out of that one?

80
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:02,840
- What?     - I can't handle two nights in a row...

81
00:05:03,010 --> 00:05:04,630
with them.

82
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:07,180
What's wrong with them?

83
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:08,970
They're just a little...

84
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:10,600
blah!

85
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:13,220
Blah?

86
00:05:13,930 --> 00:05:17,520
Well, you know, he's blah. She's just...

87
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:20,480
She's very loud for such a small person.

88
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:26,110
Uh, they're my best friends.

89
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:30,030
Are you saying we can't go out? 'Cause that'd be a problem.

90
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:32,330
Of course we can still hang out with them.

91
00:05:32,490 --> 00:05:35,200
Just not two nights in a row.

92
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,250
- Okay?     - I guess.

93
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:42,130
Thank you.

94
00:05:45,670 --> 00:05:48,630
I'll sell my friends and use the money to buy you presents.

95
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,730
- Hey.     - Hey.

96
00:05:56,930 --> 00:06:01,690
Ooh! What a great table! Where did you get it?

97
00:06:01,860 --> 00:06:03,070
Guess.

98
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:06,440
Um, a flea market?

99
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:10,820
Ha, I knew you would get it on the first guess.

100
00:06:10,990 --> 00:06:13,160
Isn't it cool? It's an apothecary table.

101
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:15,290
Ah, wow.

102
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:18,580
You can just imagine that this is where...

103
00:06:18,750 --> 00:06:21,080
they kept all their stuff to make potions.

104
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:22,290
- You know?     - Ooh, yes.

105
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:25,840
Ooh, you can almost smell the opium.

106
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:28,880
Almost.

107
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:30,380
Oh, how much was it?

108
00:06:30,550 --> 00:06:32,260
Just only 500 bucks.

109
00:06:32,430 --> 00:06:34,100
500 bucks at a flea market?

110
00:06:34,310 --> 00:06:37,930
I thought you meant how much was it...

111
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:40,020
when it was new, back then.

112
00:06:40,230 --> 00:06:41,480
- Oh, no.     - Yeah, no.

113
00:06:41,650 --> 00:06:44,690
It was at a flea market, so it was like a dollar.

114
00:06:46,530 --> 00:06:49,400
- A dollar?     - And fifty?

115
00:06:49,570 --> 00:06:52,240
So it was like one and fifty dollars.

116
00:06:52,410 --> 00:06:56,080
- They gave you the old-time pricing.     - Yeah.

117
00:06:57,410 --> 00:06:59,160
Well, what period is it from?

118
00:06:59,330 --> 00:07:00,960
Uh, it's from yore.

119
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:03,380
Like, the "days of yore," you know?

120
00:07:03,580 --> 00:07:05,750
Yes. Yes, I do.

121
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:09,210
- It's just perfect.     - Oh, good.

122
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:11,590
I bet it has a great story too.

123
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:13,890
Did they tell you where it's from or...

124
00:07:14,050 --> 00:07:16,930
Yes, that I know. This is from, uh, White Plains.

125
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:20,520
- White Plains.     - Mm-hm.

126
00:07:20,690 --> 00:07:23,600
Ooh, it sounds like such a magical place.

127
00:07:29,110 --> 00:07:30,240
- I'm here.     - Hi!

128
00:07:32,030 --> 00:07:33,320
Oh, my God!

129
00:07:33,490 --> 00:07:35,280
- You like it?     - Oh, my God!

130
00:07:35,450 --> 00:07:38,160
It's my new apothecary table!

131
00:07:38,910 --> 00:07:41,620
Phoebe'll be here any second. She can't see this.

132
00:07:41,790 --> 00:07:44,500
Why not? She'll love it. It's the real thing.

133
00:07:44,670 --> 00:07:46,460
I got it at Pottery Barn.

134
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:49,500
I know. I bought the same one.

135
00:07:49,670 --> 00:07:52,300
And if she sees your table, she'll know I lied.

136
00:07:52,470 --> 00:07:55,390
- I said ours was an original.     - Why?

137
00:07:55,550 --> 00:07:57,550
Because she hates Pottery Barn.

138
00:07:57,720 --> 00:08:01,100
She hates Pottery Barn?

139
00:08:01,850 --> 00:08:03,850
She says it's all mass-produced...

140
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:07,360
nothing is authentic, and everyone winds up having the same stuff.

141
00:08:10,820 --> 00:08:13,740
So come on! Can we cover this up, please?

142
00:08:13,910 --> 00:08:15,030
What? No, no.

143
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:17,450
I am not gonna hide it from Phoebe.

144
00:08:17,620 --> 00:08:20,330
Although I did get some great Pottery Barn sheets.

145
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:24,370
Ooh, I forgot they made sheets!

146
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:26,630
Heh, yeah.

147
00:08:28,250 --> 00:08:31,340
I still can't believe she hates Pottery Barn.

148
00:08:31,510 --> 00:08:33,050
It's not like she hates you.

149
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:35,430
Yeah, but Pottery Barn?

150
00:08:38,350 --> 00:08:40,810
You know what I think? She's just weird.

151
00:08:40,970 --> 00:08:44,350
It's because she's a twin. Twins are weird.

152
00:08:45,350 --> 00:08:49,060
She's not weird. She likes her stuff to be one-of-a-kind.

153
00:08:49,230 --> 00:08:53,820
Huh. You know what's not one-of-a-kind? A twin!

154
00:08:58,660 --> 00:09:01,370
Let's turn out the lights and watch the movie.

155
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:05,250
- Hey, ooh, cool sheet!     - You like it?

156
00:09:05,410 --> 00:09:08,210
- You want to know where I got it?     - Sure.

157
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:09,790
At a flea market.

158
00:09:11,300 --> 00:09:14,090
You buy your sheets at a flea market?

159
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:17,880
Come on, you gotta loosen the purse strings a little.

160
00:09:20,470 --> 00:09:21,510
- Hey.     - Hey.

161
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:24,060
Dinner will be ready in 20 minutes.

162
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:26,890
Here is the wine to bring over tonight.

163
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:30,360
You were also gonna buy Monica flowers...

164
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:33,480
but you couldn't because you paid for dinner last night.

165
00:09:34,860 --> 00:09:38,110
Thanks. But, uh, it's just gonna be me tonight.

166
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:42,080
- What happened to Janine?     - She's, uh, really sick.

167
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:45,790
- That's too bad.     - Yeah, she's been in there all day.

168
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:49,670
Uh, high fever, nose problem. It's...

169
00:09:49,830 --> 00:09:51,920
phlegm, phlegm, phlegm, phlegm, phlegm.

170
00:09:54,840 --> 00:09:56,340
Monica! Chandler!

171
00:09:58,260 --> 00:10:01,930
I'm sorry about tonight. I don't know if Joey told you...

172
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:05,270
but I couldn't get out of going to this play.

173
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:10,230
Have a great time, huh?

174
00:10:14,070 --> 00:10:16,570
That's funny, I saw no phlegm.

175
00:10:21,410 --> 00:10:22,990
Oh, no, she really is sick.

176
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:25,120
Then why is she going to a play?

177
00:10:25,290 --> 00:10:27,620
Uh, well, you know, "Starve a fever...

178
00:10:27,790 --> 00:10:29,710
go to a play for a cold."

179
00:10:31,330 --> 00:10:34,790
Why is Janine not coming over for dinner?

180
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:38,630
Uh, she didn't want to hang out with you guys two nights in a row.

181
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:40,470
I'm so sorry.

182
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:43,600
Why doesn't she want to hang out with us?

183
00:10:45,220 --> 00:10:48,890
Because she, uh, thinks that you are "blah."

184
00:10:51,150 --> 00:10:53,770
And that, uh, you, Monica, are too loud.

185
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:55,440
What?!

186
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:59,110
What?

187
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:01,450
So she just pretended to have a good time?

188
00:11:01,610 --> 00:11:03,030
She was lying to our faces?

189
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:05,080
I can't believe this.

190
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:06,280
Who is she to judge us?

191
00:11:06,450 --> 00:11:10,830
- We couldn't have been nicer to her.     - And I am not "blah." I am a hoot!

192
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,040
I know, I know.

193
00:11:12,210 --> 00:11:14,580
Come on, please, you guys. Don't be mad.

194
00:11:14,750 --> 00:11:16,590
I'm sure she said it...

195
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:20,050
because she was nervous 'cause you're my best friends.

196
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:22,380
And it was our first date.

197
00:11:22,550 --> 00:11:25,350
Plus, she's really sick.

198
00:11:27,430 --> 00:11:29,470
You said you made that up!

199
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:32,560
But isn't the "sick" thing better than the "play" thing?

200
00:11:33,690 --> 00:11:36,980
They're both good. I generally just go with, "Monica's drunk again."

201
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:42,530
Come on, please just give her another chance.

202
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:46,280
- She'll come around, I promise.     - Of course we will.

203
00:11:46,450 --> 00:11:49,080
- Come on, we gotta make dinner.     - Okay.

204
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:55,130
I do not like that woman!

205
00:11:55,330 --> 00:11:57,540
I can hear you!

206
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:00,090
I am loud!

207
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:05,180
Aw.

208
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:11,390
Isn't it good?

209
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:14,730
Can you please not put your feet up on my new...

210
00:12:15,230 --> 00:12:16,730
old sheet.

211
00:12:17,270 --> 00:12:18,770
Oh, sure.

212
00:12:20,150 --> 00:12:21,940
My apothecary table!

213
00:12:22,650 --> 00:12:24,740
- What?     - No!

214
00:12:25,610 --> 00:12:28,740
- Where did you get this?     - At Pottery Barn, okay?

215
00:12:31,370 --> 00:12:33,250
Oh, my God!

216
00:12:33,410 --> 00:12:37,790
Pottery Barn has ripped off the design of our antique!

217
00:12:39,170 --> 00:12:42,340
Wow. If they've ripped off our table...

218
00:12:42,510 --> 00:12:45,510
ours must be worth much more than one and fifty dollars.

219
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:50,010
This doesn't even smell like opium.

220
00:12:50,180 --> 00:12:53,180
It smells like wine, which you spilled.

221
00:12:53,350 --> 00:12:55,350
And thanks for wrecking my sheet.

222
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:59,560
Ross, calm down. I'll give you the 80 cents.

223
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:06,530
Okay, one more time.

224
00:13:06,700 --> 00:13:08,410
Would you like some orange juice?

225
00:13:08,570 --> 00:13:12,160
- Perfect decibel.     - I know!

226
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:15,460
Hey.

227
00:13:15,660 --> 00:13:17,710
Uh, what are you guys doing?

228
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:21,420
Nothing. We're just talking. You know, blah-blah-blah.

229
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:25,220
Come on, you said you were gonna try.

230
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:29,050
I came over to invite you to a movie with me and Janine.

231
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:30,760
- What do you say?     - I'd like to...

232
00:13:30,930 --> 00:13:33,350
but I'm not sure if we have the time.

233
00:13:34,680 --> 00:13:37,100
Ha, very funny. I don't know what to do.

234
00:13:37,270 --> 00:13:39,230
I really want you to get along.

235
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:42,190
Please, come to the movie with us. You owe me.

236
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:44,110
- We owe you?     - That's right.

237
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:47,360
I helped you out in the start of your relationship.

238
00:13:47,530 --> 00:13:49,740
I helped you sneak around for six months.

239
00:13:49,910 --> 00:13:51,410
I looked like an idiot.

240
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:53,410
And I was humiliated.

241
00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:55,290
And I only made $200.

242
00:13:57,330 --> 00:13:59,870
We didn't give you any money.

243
00:14:03,090 --> 00:14:04,880
You don't think I know that?

244
00:14:09,300 --> 00:14:12,550
I can't decide if it goes better by the new chair...

245
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:13,760
the Sahara desk...

246
00:14:13,930 --> 00:14:16,180
or the Parker console table.

247
00:14:16,350 --> 00:14:18,770
I didn't know there was a Pottery Barn up here.

248
00:14:20,270 --> 00:14:22,520
- I know, I went a little crazy.     - A little?

249
00:14:22,690 --> 00:14:25,570
Your place looks like page 72 of the catalog.

250
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:28,280
Look at that!

251
00:14:28,450 --> 00:14:32,570
- The ornamental birdcage! Large.     - Ha, ha.

252
00:14:35,370 --> 00:14:38,500
Oh, Ross? Be careful. That is very old.

253
00:14:38,660 --> 00:14:42,960
Colonial bird merchants used to bring their birds to market in that.

254
00:14:44,210 --> 00:14:45,500
Fascinating.

255
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:51,340
Another amazing find! Wow.

256
00:14:51,930 --> 00:14:54,970
- I bet this has a great story too.     - It does.

257
00:14:55,140 --> 00:15:00,140
It is a room-separating apparatus from Colonial times.

258
00:15:00,730 --> 00:15:04,610
Huh, a lot of this stuff is from the Colonial times.

259
00:15:04,770 --> 00:15:07,820
What are some other time periods, Rachel?

260
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:11,820
Well, there's yore.

261
00:15:13,910 --> 00:15:17,870
Ahem, and, uh, you know, yesteryear.

262
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:22,790
Rachel has such a good eye for this stuff.

263
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:25,420
Ross, if you ever decide to redecorate...

264
00:15:25,590 --> 00:15:28,050
and I think you should...

265
00:15:28,210 --> 00:15:29,960
you should ask Rachel to help.

266
00:15:30,130 --> 00:15:31,670
Aw, he doesn't need my help.

267
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:35,140
I think he's ready to get rid of the... What'd you call it?

268
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:37,640
The, uh, "cheap knockoffs and dinosaur junk"?

269
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:44,270
Really?

270
00:15:45,310 --> 00:15:49,530
You know, since you have such a great sense of what I need...

271
00:15:49,690 --> 00:15:51,570
Uh, here's 60 bucks.

272
00:15:51,740 --> 00:15:55,700
Why don't you take Phoebe down to that Colonial, uh, flea market...

273
00:15:55,870 --> 00:15:57,410
and get me some stuff?

274
00:16:02,290 --> 00:16:04,580
I don't think Phoebe wants to come.

275
00:16:04,750 --> 00:16:06,580
- I do want to.     - She does want to.

276
00:16:06,750 --> 00:16:08,250
- She does want to.     - Yeah!

277
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:18,260
I guess the flea market was just better last time.

278
00:16:18,430 --> 00:16:21,350
Well, at least I got these sheets for Ross.

279
00:16:22,730 --> 00:16:25,400
Ugh, look at this. Pottery Barn.

280
00:16:25,560 --> 00:16:29,110
Ugh, yeah, you know what? Don't look at it.

281
00:16:29,270 --> 00:16:31,780
- Seriously, don't look at it.     - Look!

282
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:34,150
There's the table they stole from us.

283
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:36,280
Oh, those bastards! Let's go.

284
00:16:37,830 --> 00:16:42,450
That fan kind of looks like ours, and the birdcage, and...

285
00:16:42,830 --> 00:16:45,790
Wait a second, this is our exact living room!

286
00:16:45,960 --> 00:16:48,130
No, it's not.

287
00:16:48,290 --> 00:16:50,630
Come on, ours is totally different.

288
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:54,590
I mean, we don't have the... We don't have that lamp.

289
00:16:55,340 --> 00:16:59,760
And that screen is on the other side.

290
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:03,930
Oh, my God. This is where you got all our stuff.

291
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:07,100
- Pottery Barn! Oh, my God!     - No. No.

292
00:17:07,270 --> 00:17:09,730
Okay, I did, all right? I'm sorry.

293
00:17:09,900 --> 00:17:13,990
I wanted this stuff and I know how you feel about Pottery Barn.

294
00:17:14,150 --> 00:17:17,070
- Come on, don't be mad.     - No, but I am mad.

295
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:20,320
Because this stuff is what's wrong with the world...

296
00:17:20,490 --> 00:17:22,870
and it's in my living room...

297
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:27,000
and all I can think about is how I don't have that lamp!

298
00:17:28,210 --> 00:17:30,130
Well, then, honey, buy the lamp.

299
00:17:30,290 --> 00:17:32,630
We have that 60 bucks from Ross.

300
00:17:34,420 --> 00:17:36,970
I can't! I can't!

301
00:17:37,470 --> 00:17:39,090
Unless...

302
00:17:39,260 --> 00:17:43,100
Are you saying you'd move out if I didn't buy that lamp?

303
00:17:43,270 --> 00:17:45,350
No, I'm not gonna move out!

304
00:17:45,520 --> 00:17:49,480
But are you saying that you would move out if I didn't buy that lamp?

305
00:17:50,940 --> 00:17:54,280
- Yes! I would so move out.     - Then I don't have a choice.

306
00:17:54,440 --> 00:17:57,820
- I have to buy that lamp!     - That's right.

307
00:17:58,530 --> 00:18:00,820
But at least the apothecary table's real.

308
00:18:06,370 --> 00:18:09,920
This was great. Didn't everybody have a great time?

309
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:12,750
I did. I really did. And I've got to say...

310
00:18:12,920 --> 00:18:16,460
I'm sorry if I was weird after the last time we went out.

311
00:18:16,630 --> 00:18:18,380
I guess I was just nervous.

312
00:18:18,550 --> 00:18:21,430
- That's understandable.     - Don't worry about it.

313
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:22,850
So we can go out again?

314
00:18:23,010 --> 00:18:24,640
- Absolutely.     - Well, good.

315
00:18:24,810 --> 00:18:27,060
- Okay, well, good night.     - Good night.

316
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:33,270
See, huh?

317
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:34,690
Wasn't that fun?

318
00:18:34,860 --> 00:18:36,780
We have got to move.

319
00:18:37,530 --> 00:18:38,990
What?

320
00:18:39,150 --> 00:18:40,490
I knew it!

321
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:44,700
You're not so quiet yourself, missy!

322
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:49,580
I'm blah? The only thing more boring than modern dance...

323
00:18:49,750 --> 00:18:51,120
is you talking about it.

324
00:18:51,290 --> 00:18:52,750
Oh, Chandler...

325
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:54,960
I just lost myself in the movement."

326
00:18:58,170 --> 00:19:00,340
You know, I know you're talking...

327
00:19:00,510 --> 00:19:03,340
but all I hear is blah-blah-blah-blah-blah.

328
00:19:03,510 --> 00:19:06,890
All right, you and me! Let's go, right now!

329
00:19:07,060 --> 00:19:08,390
All right, enough!

330
00:19:09,230 --> 00:19:12,600
Enough! You two, go home. I gotta talk to Janine.

331
00:19:12,770 --> 00:19:15,230
I really think you could take her.

332
00:19:15,730 --> 00:19:17,980
You better hope I don't see you in the hallway!

333
00:19:23,410 --> 00:19:25,120
All right. Uh...

334
00:19:25,280 --> 00:19:27,990
Look, we got a little bit of a problem here.

335
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:30,620
You can't my treat my friends that way.

336
00:19:30,790 --> 00:19:35,380
- They said stuff to me too.     - I know, I'll talk to them about it.

337
00:19:35,540 --> 00:19:37,250
They mean so much to me.

338
00:19:37,420 --> 00:19:39,710
They're like my family, you know?

339
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:43,050
If you guys are gonna be fighting all the time...

340
00:19:44,430 --> 00:19:48,310
I don't think we can be together. It just can't work, okay?

341
00:19:48,470 --> 00:19:49,640
It can't.

342
00:19:49,810 --> 00:19:51,600
I'm very upset.

343
00:19:53,230 --> 00:19:54,810
Okay.

344
00:19:54,980 --> 00:19:57,270
Would it help if I apologized?

345
00:19:57,480 --> 00:19:59,150
Yeah.

346
00:19:59,320 --> 00:20:01,110
That'd be very helpful.

347
00:20:06,070 --> 00:20:08,780
What did I tell you about the hall?

348
00:20:10,870 --> 00:20:13,870
I was coming over to apologize for my behavior.

349
00:20:15,460 --> 00:20:18,090
I'd really like it if we could be friends.

350
00:20:20,670 --> 00:20:24,930
Well, I know that would make Joey happy, so...

351
00:20:25,090 --> 00:20:27,220
I would like that too.

352
00:20:28,350 --> 00:20:30,890
- Great.     - Now, come on.

353
00:20:33,980 --> 00:20:36,350
- I'm glad we worked it out.     - Me too.

354
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:38,480
- I'll see you.     - Bye.

355
00:20:38,650 --> 00:20:41,820
- Or I'll hear you.     - That's it, big girl!

356
00:20:43,860 --> 00:20:45,490
Wait!

357
00:20:49,080 --> 00:20:51,240
You'd better run!

358
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:54,120
Did you hear that?

359
00:20:55,290 --> 00:20:57,960
- What am I gonna do?     - I'm sorry, man.

360
00:21:00,300 --> 00:21:01,840
- Want to go watch?     - Yeah!

361
00:21:10,470 --> 00:21:11,510
- Hey.     - Hey.

362
00:21:11,680 --> 00:21:14,390
- How you doing?     - So, uh, did she move out?

363
00:21:14,560 --> 00:21:16,390
Pretty much.

364
00:21:16,560 --> 00:21:18,940
I can't believe you just broke up with her.

365
00:21:19,110 --> 00:21:21,190
When it's not right, you know it.

366
00:21:21,360 --> 00:21:24,610
- You okay?     - I'll be all right.

367
00:21:25,860 --> 00:21:28,950
- You know what'd cheer you up?     - What?

368
00:21:29,160 --> 00:21:33,080
I'm giving this lecture on erosion theories tomorrow night.

369
00:21:35,410 --> 00:21:37,160
I think you should come.

370
00:21:44,050 --> 00:21:46,300
You're right, that did cheer me up.

